    <text>
        <body>
            <div xml:id="d427">

                <pb facs="0590" n="446" sameAs="frus:frus1929v02/pg446"/>

                <note rend="inline" type="source">393.1163Am3/52: Telegram</note>
                <head><hi rendition="#italic">The Minister in China</hi> (<hi
                            rendition="#italic">MacMurray</hi>) <hi rendition="#italic"
                        >to the Secretary of State</hi></head>
                <opener><dateline rendition="#float-right"><placeName><hi rendition="#small-caps"
                                >Peking</hi></placeName>, <date calendar="gregorian"
                            when="1929-04-01T19:00:00+08:00">April 1, 1929—7
                        p.m.</date><lb/>[Received April 2—4:20 p.m.<anchor n="50" xml:id="d427-f1"
                        />]</dateline></opener>
                <p>254. Department’s No. 104, March 23 [<hi rendition="#italic">26</hi>], 7 p.m.,
                    was repeated to the American Consuls at Nanking, Shanghai, Hankow, and Canton
                    and telegraphic note was addressed to Ministry of Foreign Affairs at Nanking.
                    …</p>
                <p rendition="#center">. . . . . . . . . . . . . .</p>
                <p>The following reply has been received from the American Consul at Nanking: <quote
                        rend="blockquote">
                        <p>“March 31, noon. Yesterday afternoon I presented to Vice Minister Tong a
                            memorandum summarizing the Legation’s March 28, 10 p.m. He said that the
                            Government had had no report on the matter; that communications with
                            Kiangsi seemed to be broken but somehow the Government would take all
                            steps possible to protect American life and property there. He pointed
                            out that the region affected was in the zone between contending Kwangsi
                            [and] Nanking armies and was held by strong, well-organized Communist
                            forces. …”</p>
                    </quote></p>
                <closer><signed><hi rendition="#small-caps">MacMurray</hi></signed></closer>
            </div>
        </body>
    </text>
    <standOff>
        <listAnnotation>
